    mechanics: 'Processors are applied in a fixed order per engine. Penalties come first and depend on the sequence so far: a repetition penalty divides (or multiplies, for negative logits) the logit of every token already generated by a factor `> 1`; presence penalty subtracts a constant from tokens that have appeared; frequency penalty subtracts a constant times the count. Bias terms (`logit_bias`) add per-token offsets, and banned tokens are set to `−∞`. Temperature divides the logits: `z / τ`, with `τ → 0` approaching greedy and `τ > 1` flattening the distribution. Top-k keeps the k largest logits; top-p (nucleus) keeps the smallest set whose softmax mass exceeds `p`; min-p keeps tokens whose probability is at least `p × max_prob`; everything else is masked to `−∞`. Structured output masks come last: a grammar or JSON-schema engine tracks the automaton state per sequence and masks every token that would not be a valid continuation, which guarantees syntactically valid output at the cost of per-step mask computation. Because processors are per request but the logits are batched, engines vectorize them by grouping requests with identical settings or by applying dense per-row parameter tensors on the GPU.',
