    mechanics: 'Naive per-token decode is wrong for two reasons: byte-level BPE tokens can end in the middle of a UTF-8 sequence, and SentencePiece-style tokenizers attach leading-space markers whose rendering depends on the neighbouring token. Engines therefore keep, per sequence, the ids already emitted and a prefix offset: at each step they decode the tail window of ids to text, compare with the previously emitted text, and emit only the new, complete characters, holding back any trailing bytes that form an incomplete code point (which surface as a replacement character if decoded early). Byte-fallback tokens (raw bytes for characters outside the vocabulary) are accumulated until they form a valid code point. Special tokens such as end-of-turn are stripped from the text unless the client asks for them. Stop strings, unlike stop token ids, can span token boundaries and are only detectable on the decoded text, so the engine buffers enough text to check for them and truncates the output at the match. Detokenization is CPU work per sequence per step and is typically done in a separate process or thread pool so that it overlaps with the next GPU step.',
