    mechanics: 'After each decode iteration, the detokenized delta for every streaming sequence is wrapped in an event, for the OpenAI-compatible shape a `chat.completion.chunk` object with a `delta.content` string and a `finish_reason` that is null until the last chunk, and written as `data: {json}\\n\\n` on the SSE stream; the stream ends with a `data: [DONE]` sentinel. Chunks may be coalesced when multiple tokens are produced in one iteration (speculative decoding) or when the detokenizer holds back incomplete characters. Tool-call arguments and structured outputs are streamed as partial JSON deltas the client assembles. The gateway must flush each event immediately and avoid response buffering in proxies, otherwise TTFT as observed by the client degrades even though the engine is fast. When the client disconnects, the gateway signals the engine to abort the request; the engine removes it from the running batch at the next iteration and frees its blocks, so cancelled requests do not keep consuming decode budget. A final chunk (or trailer) carries usage: prompt tokens, completion tokens, and often cached prompt tokens, so billing and capacity accounting agree with what the engine actually processed. Non-streaming requests follow the same engine path and simply buffer until completion.',
